'''Jayson Christopher Tatum Sr.''' ({{IPAc-en |ˈ|t|eɪ|t|ə|m}} {{respell|TAY|təm}}; born March 3, 1998) is an American professional [[basketball]] player for the [[Boston Celtics]] of the [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA). He was a [[McDonald's All-American Game|McDonald's All-American]] in high school in [[Missouri]] and played [[college basketball]] for the [[Duke Blue Devils men's basketball|Duke Blue Devils]]. Tatum was selected by the [[Boston Celtics]] with the third overall pick in the [[2017 NBA draft]] and was voted to the [[NBA All-Rookie First Team]] in the [[2017–18 NBA season|2018 season]]. Tatum won the inaugural [[NBA conference finals MVP awards|NBA Eastern Conference finals MVP]] in [[2022 NBA playoffs|2022]], and won his first title in the [[2024 NBA Finals]].

Nicknamed "'''the Anomaly'''",<ref>{{cite web|last=Flores|first=Geca|url=https://sports.yahoo.com/article/jayson-tatums-reaction-strange-nickname-013416640.html|title=Jayson Tatum's Reaction to Strange Nickname Leaves Travis Kelce, Jason Kelce in Stitches|work=[[Athlon Sports]]|via=[[Yahoo! Sports]]|date=March 26, 2025|accessdate=June 20, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Roche|first=Conor|url=https://www.boston.com/sports/boston-celtics/2025/03/09/jayson-tatum-nickname-the-anomaly-player-a-big-deuce/|title=Jayson Tatum settles nickname debate following emergence of 'The Anomaly'|website=[[Boston.com]]|date=March 9, 2025|accessdate=June 20, 2025}}</ref> Tatum is a six-time [[NBA All-Star]] and five-time [[All-NBA Team|All-NBA selection]] (including four first-team selections). He and [[Jaylen Brown]] make up the Celtics' "[[Jays (Boston Celtics)|Jays]]" duo. In 2024, Tatum led the team to a league-best 64 wins and a championship in the [[2024 NBA Finals|NBA Finals]]. Tatum also won a gold medal with the [[2020 United States men's Olympic basketball team|2020]] and [[2024 United States men's Olympic basketball team|2024 U.S. Olympic team]]s.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.olympics.com/en/paris-2024/athlete/jayson-tatum_1954684 |title=TATUM Jayson |work=[[Paris 2024 Olympics]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241007153124/https://olympics.com/en/paris-2024/athlete/jayson-tatum_1954684 |archive-date=October 7, 2024}}</ref> He holds the records for most points scored in an [[NBA All-Star Game]] (55), most points scored in a [[Game seven|Game 7]] of any [[NBA playoffs]] series (51), and most points scored in the [[NBA playoffs]] at his age.
